From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 02:37:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 23/27] s2r: suspend-to-RAM warm boot mailbox and resume path
 for T8132 (J604)

Give Linux's apple-s2r driver the firmware half it needs on the J604:
a warm boot mailbox published in /chosen/asahi,s2r-mailbox, and a path
from the core reset after a suspend-to-RAM wake back into the kernel.

Where the wake lands (SOURCE, aura PR #109, J713 T8132 trace): the wake
is the first core reset after the suspend-to-RAM commit, at the core's
locked RVBAR, MMU off; no iBoot step runs. On T8132 the locked RVBAR is
the _vectors_start of the m1n1 image that ran kboot (_base + 0x10000 in
this build): smp.c starts a secondary only when RVBAR equals this image's
_vectors_start or its pre-move copy, chainload copies each stage over the
previous stage's _base (CODE, smp.c, chainload.c), and kboot reserves
_base.._end. So the mailbox lives in this image's .data and the
check sits at the top of _cpu_reset_c (both TO-VERIFY on a J604 with the
read-only probe on the J604: RVBAR of the boot core,
memreserve covering it).

_cpu_reset_c, on the boot core only, before any output: if the mailbox
holds the magic, s2r_warm_resume() disarms it, sets the resumed flag
(bit 63), stops the primary watchdog at the base found at boot (the ADT
is gone by then), redoes init_cpu() with its output suppressed (the UARTs
and USB are SoC-domain devices that are still off), and branches to the
kernel's entry (arm64 cpu_resume) at EL2 with the MMU and caches off,
x0 = arg. The bring-up flags reboot through the watchdog at entry or
right before the branch. The cluster frequency and memory cache requests
(flags 1 and 2) are not honoured: they need the ADT.

Published only on T8132; other SoCs are unchanged. The mailbox stays
inert until a kernel arms it (apple_s2r.enable=1). Not yet tested on
hardware.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: LLM
